Subject: The prototype epoch is the watch for a prototype's data-property value overwrites

What this does
  - Closes the three stores into an existing data property of a prototype that did not move the
    prototype epoch: the interpreter's direct-slot put_field hit (both register-VM entry points)
    and `defineProperty` / `JS_DefinePropertyValue` updating the value of an existing data property.
  - Adds `qjs_aot_proto_method`, which a unit asks for the bytecode function a receiver shape's
    prototype holds under an atom. It refuses a holder without the prototype flag.

Why we need it
  A compiled call site that the profile saw reach one prototype method can treat the method as a
  constant for as long as the prototype epoch has not moved. THE EPOCH IS NOW THE WATCH for every
  overwrite of a prototype's data property: no bump below is redundant, and none may be dropped.
  The set-property slow path, the unit's own direct store, and the accessor / read-only / delete /
  add / prototype-swap paths already moved it; these three did not.

Functions touched
  `JS_DefineProperty` (the epoch condition), `regvm_t_put_field`, the `ROP_put_field` arm of
  `js_regvm_execute`, new `qjs_aot_proto_method`.

No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -16588 +16588 @@
-            ((flags & (JS_PROP_HAS_GET | JS_PROP_HAS_SET)) ||
+            ((flags & (JS_PROP_HAS_GET | JS_PROP_HAS_SET | JS_PROP_HAS_VALUE)) ||
@@ -31557,0 +31558,6 @@
+                /* A direct slot store bypasses JS_SetPropertyInternal2, so it
+                   moves the prototype epoch itself. */
+#if JS_ENABLE_IC
+                if (unlikely(p->is_prototype))
+                    ic_watchpoint_bump(ctx->rt);
+#endif
@@ -35206,0 +35213,4 @@
+#if JS_ENABLE_IC
+                            if (unlikely(p->is_prototype))
+                                ic_watchpoint_bump(ctx->rt);
+#endif
@@ -64481,0 +64492,25 @@
+}
+
+/* The bytecode function object that the prototype of a receiver shape holds under
+   `atom` as a plain data property, or NULL (an accessor, an exotic prototype, one without the
+   prototype flag, a value that is not a bytecode function). Borrowed, and it runs no JS and allocates nothing.
+   The unit takes its own reference before it keeps the pointer, and asks again whenever
+   the prototype epoch moves. */
+QJS_AOT_STUB void *qjs_aot_proto_method(const void *recv_shape, uint32_t atom)
+{
+    const JSObject *h = ((const JSShape *)recv_shape)->proto;
+    JSProperty *pr;
+    JSShapeProperty *prs;
+    JSValue v;
+
+    /* The epoch guarantee rests on the holder carrying the prototype flag. */
+    if (h == NULL || h->is_exotic || !h->is_prototype)
+        return NULL;
+    prs = find_own_property(&pr, (JSObject *)h, (JSAtom)atom);
+    if (prs == NULL || (prs->flags & (JS_PROP_TMASK | JS_PROP_LENGTH)))
+        return NULL;
+    v = pr->u.value;
+    if (JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(v) != JS_TAG_OBJECT ||
+        JS_VALUE_GET_OBJ(v)->class_id != JS_CLASS_BYTECODE_FUNCTION)
+        return NULL;
+    return JS_VALUE_GET_OBJ(v);
--- a/quickjs-aot-rt.h
+++ b/quickjs-aot-rt.h
@@ -322,0 +323 @@
+QJS_AOT_STUB void *qjs_aot_proto_method(const void *recv_shape, uint32_t atom);
